    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the expression \\((1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)\\), let's use the tangent addition formula. The tangent addition formula states that for any angles \\(A\\) and \\(B\\),\n\n\\[\n\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\tan B}.\n\\]\n\nIf we set \\(A = 20^\\circ\\) and \\(B = 25^\\circ\\), then we have:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\tan 45^\\circ = 1.\n\\]\n\nUsing the tangent addition formula, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 45^\\circ = \\frac{\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ}{1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ} = 1.\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nRearranging terms, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1.\n\\]\n\nNow, let's consider the expression \\(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ\\). The left side can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[\n1 + (\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ) + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1 + 1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 2 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ.\n\\]\n\nHowever, a simpler approach is to recognize that the expression can be factored as follows:\n\n\\[\n(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ) = 1 + \\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ + \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = (\\tan 20^\\circ + \\tan 25^\\circ) + 1 - \\tan 20^\\circ \\tan 25^\\circ = 1.\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}.\n\\]",
